course: dictionary
lesson: 7426
title: Politeness and manners
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Politeness means behaving with good manners and respect for other people. Saying please and thank you, waiting your turn, and not interrupting are all examples. «Politeness never comes before your safety.» Being polite never means you must stay with someone who makes you feel unsafe, and you can always tell a trusted adult.
explain: In stories, a polite character often wins help or makes friends. Politeness can also be quiet and simple, like holding a door open for someone or saying sorry when you bump into them.
explain: Ways of being polite change from place to place. «Politeness sounds different in different places.» A shop in London may say "Cheers, love", and one in Glasgow may say "Cheers, hen". None is more correct than the others; they are all kind.
fact: Politeness is a noun. You can show politeness, or you can be polite.
fact: «Out of politeness» means you do something because good manners ask you to.
fact: «With great politeness» means very respectfully and kindly.
fact: «Simple politeness» means the basic good manners everyone should use, like saying please.
fact: Politeness rules differ between countries and families, but the idea of showing respect is shared by all of them.
exercise: «Politeness never comes before your safety.» What does this sentence mean? | Being polite matters more than being safe | Politeness and safety are the same thing | Safety only matters at school | *Your safety always matters more than being polite
exercise: Lily holds the door open for the man with the heavy shopping. Why does she do it? | *Out of politeness | Because she is in a hurry to leave | Because she is angry with him | To make the door close faster
exercise: A shopkeeper in Manchester says "Ta, love" as you pay. What does this show? | A question about your name | *A polite word of thanks | A request to leave the shop | A warning about the weather
test: Which phrase means doing something with very respectful and kind behaviour? | Politeness never comes before | *With great politeness | Simple politeness | Out of politeness
test: Fill the gap: Sam spoke to the old lady with great ___. | *politeness | hurry | sadness | loudness
exam: Which statement about politeness is true? | *Politeness can look different in different places, but it always shows respect | Politeness is the same in every country and family | Politeness is only needed at school | Politeness means being rude when you are in a hurry
